   "html_text": "This article has examined the ongoing interpretive debate concerning Kant’s conception of the contribution of the sensible and intellectual faculties of the mind to a subject’s sensory experience of the world around it. Two central motivations of the debate are, on the one hand, philosophical concerns about the epistemological relationship between sensory experience (broadly construed) and empirical judgment or belief, and on the other hand, interpretive questions concerning Kant’s conception of",
   "md_text": "This article has examined the ongoing interpretive debate concerning Kant’s conception of the contribution of the sensible and intellectual faculties of the mind to a subject’s sensory experience of the world around it. Two central motivations of the debate are, on the one hand, philosophical concerns about the epistemological relationship between sensory experience (broadly construed) and empirical judgment or belief, and on the other hand, interpretive questions concerning Kant’s conception of"
  },
  {
   "i": 37,
   "shape_h": "ol",
   "shape_m": "ol",
   "html_text": "The content of a cognition (whether intuition or concept) consists in—i.e., is nothing but—a relation to an object Concepts and intuitions relate to objects in different ways—viz. mediately and immediately ∴ The content of intuition is different from the content of concepts Representations are individuated as to type—i.e., intuition or concept—at least partly in virtue of their content ∴ Intuitions cannot have conceptual content",
